xnu 0399: the HCI control bluetoothd's POSIX driver opens

0386 put the HCI transport behind /dev/cu.bluetooth on the strength of the
access() pair and the termios imports in bluetoothd. The disassembly of
/usr/sbin/bluetoothd (16.7.16) says otherwise: BT_HCI_TRANSPORT parses as
"transport:driver[:parameter]", and the POSIX driver takes the parameter as
the kernel control name it resolves with CTLIOCGINFO at 0x100066d34.

That name selects the data path. With exactly "com.apple.uart.sk.bluetooth"
the driver asks for a 16-byte UUID (0x40105412) and feeds it to
os_channel_create(); this kernel has no Skywalk (SKYWALK_BASE is not built), so
the call returns NULL and the driver closes the socket. It does not fall back -
measured 2026-11-20, errno ENOENT and "Failed to initialize driver POSIX". With
any other control name the same driver takes the plain path at 0x100066e50: it
sets the termios and the 12-byte UART option, leaves its channel pointer NULL,
and writes and reads the control socket directly (0x1000676a4, 0x100067a9c).

This registers the control as com.mondrian.btuart; the shim passes that name as
the third token, BT_HCI_TRANSPORT=H4:POSIX:com.mondrian.btuart. It forwards
write(2) to
the existing QUP wrapper 2 engine and polls the engine every 10 ms into
ctl_enqueuedata(), the same rate the tty used: 115 bytes at 115200 fit the
128-byte RX FIFO. The termios and UART options the daemon sets are accepted
and printed; the engine stays at the measured 14745600 Hz root and 8N1.

The control is registered from the root-mount probe block, next to
mondrian_bt_tty_init(), before launchd starts bluetoothd. /dev/cu.bluetooth
stays, because FindLocalMacAddress looks for it before the control is used.

